1
00:00:06,172 --> 00:00:07,882
[children's melody plays]

2
00:00:13,513 --> 00:00:16,015
-[cash register chimes]
-Here's your receipt and your keys.

3
00:00:16,099 --> 00:00:17,892
Thank you.
I've always wanted one of these.

4
00:00:17,976 --> 00:00:19,936
-Would you like a bag?
-Sure.

5
00:00:23,606 --> 00:00:24,733
How am I gonna get this home?

6
00:00:26,151 --> 00:00:27,444
I'll drive it.

7
00:00:28,528 --> 00:00:30,029
[engine turns over, revs]

8
00:00:50,508 --> 00:00:53,053
[Jerry] This is a 1991 Nissan Figaro

9
00:00:53,136 --> 00:00:54,429
in pale aqua

10
00:00:54,512 --> 00:00:55,972
with an ivory interior.

11
00:00:56,347 --> 00:00:57,390
Right-hand drive.

12
00:00:58,099 --> 00:00:59,726
You know, when you start out in comedy,

13
00:00:59,809 --> 00:01:01,561
you don't really know
how it's going to work.

14
00:01:01,853 --> 00:01:04,105
It's just something you decide
you want to do,

15
00:01:04,314 --> 00:01:06,983
and you assume you'll figure it out
as you go along.

16
00:01:07,358 --> 00:01:09,319
And that seemed to be the thinking 
behind this car

17
00:01:09,402 --> 00:01:11,738
when Nissan produced them
back in the early '90s.

18
00:01:11,821 --> 00:01:14,657
They thought, "Let's just make
something cute that we like

19
00:01:14,741 --> 00:01:17,118
and maybe somebody else out there
will like it."

20
00:01:17,202 --> 00:01:18,912
[ship horn blares]

21
00:01:19,496 --> 00:01:21,831
It has a little engine. It's a little car.

22
00:01:21,915 --> 00:01:23,249
It has a little folding top.

23
00:01:23,958 --> 00:01:25,960
-It only weighs 1,800 pounds.
-[horn toots]

24
00:01:26,044 --> 00:01:29,005
Everything about this car
has a cute, fun, happy,

25
00:01:29,089 --> 00:01:31,549
friendly, silly, pointless vibe about it.

26
00:01:31,841 --> 00:01:33,176
It even says right on the car,

27
00:01:33,301 --> 00:01:35,762
"Ideal for the person
who enjoys savoring life."

28
00:01:35,845 --> 00:01:39,390
Isn't it amazing how well the Japanese
are able to market stuff to us

29
00:01:39,724 --> 00:01:41,810
while really having no idea
who we really are?

30
00:01:42,018 --> 00:01:43,478
We're not savoring life!

31
00:01:45,021 --> 00:01:48,024
Anyway, this car reminds me a lot
of my very special guest today,

32
00:01:48,107 --> 00:01:51,236
Saturday Night Live star
Melissa Villaseñor.

33
00:01:51,569 --> 00:01:52,904
She does lots of cute voices.

34
00:01:52,987 --> 00:01:54,072
Wah, wah.

35
00:01:54,155 --> 00:01:54,989
Rope her in.

36
00:01:55,073 --> 00:01:57,700
-I think Melissa's crazy!
-[Jerry] She wears cute clothes.

37
00:01:57,784 --> 00:01:59,452
Really cute outfit. Fanny pack!

38
00:01:59,536 --> 00:02:01,037
-Seem like a match.
-Here we go.

39
00:02:01,121 --> 00:02:03,832
[speaking Spanish in a high-pitched voice]

40
00:02:03,915 --> 00:02:05,208
[audience laughs]

41
00:02:07,001 --> 00:02:09,003
-[Melissa] Is this the big call?
-[Jerry] This is it.

42
00:02:09,087 --> 00:02:11,297
Remember when you thought
when you get into show business 

43
00:02:11,381 --> 00:02:13,049
that there's gonna be some big calls?

44
00:02:13,132 --> 00:02:14,425
-The phone's gonna ring.
-Right.

45
00:02:14,509 --> 00:02:16,678
-And you're life's gonna change, right?
-[Melissa] Yeah.

46
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:20,140
-[Jerry] This is one of those calls.
-[Melissa] I'm ready for coffee, Seinfeld.

47
00:02:20,223 --> 00:02:24,561
[Jerry] Me too. I'm very excited
to spend some time with you today.

48
00:02:24,644 --> 00:02:26,312
I'm bored out of my mind.

49
00:02:26,396 --> 00:02:29,232
I need to talk with someone who's off.

50
00:02:29,315 --> 00:02:30,900
[Melissa] Yes. I'm off all the time.

51
00:02:31,401 --> 00:02:33,736
-[Jerry] Let's get coffee!
-[Melissa] Let's get coffee!

52
00:02:34,612 --> 00:02:36,614
Why are we still doing the phone call?

53
00:02:36,698 --> 00:02:38,449
-It's part of our gig.
-[Jerry laughs]

54
00:02:42,579 --> 00:02:45,456
[Jerry] Let's find out
with me, Jerry Seinfeld.

55
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:48,501
And this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

56
00:02:53,923 --> 00:02:56,050
-[gasps] This is for me?
-Yeah.

57
00:02:56,134 --> 00:02:58,595
Oh, man! How cute!

58
00:02:58,678 --> 00:03:01,681
-What do you think of it?
-Oh, I love it! This is awesome!

59
00:03:03,766 --> 00:03:04,684
[Melissa laughs]

60
00:03:04,767 --> 00:03:07,729
[Melissa] This feels like a dream.
This doesn't seem real right now.

61
00:03:09,230 --> 00:03:11,316
I like how--
This is for the air conditioning?

62
00:03:11,399 --> 00:03:12,609
For your face and foot.

63
00:03:12,692 --> 00:03:15,236
-[Jerry] Not feet. Foot.
-[Melissa] Foot. [laughs]

64
00:03:16,487 --> 00:03:18,031
[Melissa] Where are we going for coffee?

65
00:03:18,114 --> 00:03:19,824
-Is this a surprise?
-[Jerry] Yeah.

66
00:03:20,116 --> 00:03:22,118
[Melissa] This is the best day of my life.

67
00:03:22,202 --> 00:03:23,203
[Melissa laughs]

68
00:03:24,746 --> 00:03:26,956
Everybody's got a hair thing in Brooklyn.

69
00:03:27,498 --> 00:03:29,876
A hair thing, a shoe thing, a jean thing.

70
00:03:33,713 --> 00:03:38,051
It's a circus sideshow, and I felt so bad.
I know they were happy, but...

71
00:03:38,134 --> 00:03:40,386
People in the circus sideshow,

72
00:03:40,470 --> 00:03:43,139
huge, fat, tattooed...

73
00:03:43,723 --> 00:03:46,267
These are the people in the audience now.

74
00:03:47,143 --> 00:03:50,146
What used to be the sideshow
is just regular people now.

75
00:03:50,230 --> 00:03:52,148
-[laughs]
-They're not that different...

76
00:03:52,232 --> 00:03:55,234
-They're not that special.
-No, they're not freakish at all.

77
00:03:55,318 --> 00:03:56,986
[Melissa laughs]

78
00:03:59,155 --> 00:04:01,866
[siren wails in the distance]

79
00:04:04,369 --> 00:04:06,955
[Jerry] This looks like some kind
of food museum place.

80
00:04:07,038 --> 00:04:08,748
-[Melissa] Yeah.
-Let's see what it is.

81
00:04:16,214 --> 00:04:19,133
-Oh, this is the fortune cookie machine.
-[Melissa] Cool.

82
00:04:19,217 --> 00:04:21,719
[Jerry] Look, that's how
the fortunes go in!

83
00:04:21,803 --> 00:04:23,096
[machine whirrs]

84
00:04:23,471 --> 00:04:26,140
"Man without food is man without heart."

85
00:04:28,101 --> 00:04:29,519
-Hi, Peter. 
-Hi. It's a pleasure.

86
00:04:29,602 --> 00:04:30,937
Why did you do this?

87
00:04:31,145 --> 00:04:33,272
Yeah, well, there isn't any museum
anywhere in the world

88
00:04:33,356 --> 00:04:35,817
that actually connects food
to culture, history, and science.

89
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:38,194
But, yeah, so this is actually a machine
that was used

90
00:04:38,277 --> 00:04:39,988
to make breakfast cereal for decades.

91
00:04:40,071 --> 00:04:43,032
It's called a puffing gun. It actually
fires the cereal out like a cannon.

92
00:04:43,116 --> 00:04:44,867
-Wow!
-[Melissa] You like cereal, don't you?

93
00:04:44,951 --> 00:04:46,661
-[Jerry] I love cereal.
-[Melissa] I do, too.

94
00:04:46,744 --> 00:04:48,830
[Peter] The technology came about
at a time when cereal

95
00:04:48,913 --> 00:04:52,041
was actually transitioning
from a cure for masturbation...

96
00:04:52,125 --> 00:04:54,711
-Oh, my-- I am sorry about that.
-Oh, Jesus. Seinfeld...

97
00:04:54,794 --> 00:04:57,547
-Let me apologize in advance.
-[Melissa laughs]

98
00:04:57,630 --> 00:05:00,925
-It takes two hands for cereal, you know?
-[Melissa] Right. [laughs]

99
00:05:01,301 --> 00:05:04,971
You're pouring it.
You got the milk and the spoon.

100
00:05:05,388 --> 00:05:07,598
And you're getting the prize.

101
00:05:07,682 --> 00:05:09,642
So, it keeps your hands busy.

102
00:05:09,892 --> 00:05:11,269
Maybe that's how it works.

103
00:05:15,315 --> 00:05:17,108
-What is your name?
-My name is John.

104
00:05:17,191 --> 00:05:20,653
Um... so, that's real MSG?
I've never seen that before.

105
00:05:20,737 --> 00:05:24,198
-[John] I'm gonna put it on your food.
-And they put it in for flavor?

106
00:05:24,282 --> 00:05:25,950
-[John] Um...
-And it'll make you crazy.

107
00:05:26,034 --> 00:05:27,744
It makes food delicious.

108
00:05:27,827 --> 00:05:29,120
[sizzling]

109
00:05:29,203 --> 00:05:32,123
We are in a very strange place.

110
00:05:32,582 --> 00:05:35,209
-We're eating in a museum of food.
-[Melissa laughs]

111
00:05:35,293 --> 00:05:36,794
This is really weird.

112
00:05:36,878 --> 00:05:39,380
-[Melissa] I've never experienced this.
-No! No one has.

113
00:05:39,922 --> 00:05:40,965
It's insane.

114
00:05:41,466 --> 00:05:44,469
He's part of the museum.
Do you understand how crazy this is?

115
00:05:44,552 --> 00:05:47,722
-[laughing] Yeah, I guess you're right.
-We're not in a restaurant.

116
00:05:47,805 --> 00:05:49,432
-[laughing]
-We're in a museum.

117
00:05:49,932 --> 00:05:50,767
[Melissa laughs]

118
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:53,394
-Why do people keep changing things?
-[Melissa laughs]

119
00:05:54,812 --> 00:05:56,481
So, there'll be a tremendous...

120
00:05:57,106 --> 00:06:00,985
uncomfortable attention on you,
as you eat...

121
00:06:01,944 --> 00:06:03,613
because it's not a restaurant.

122
00:06:03,696 --> 00:06:04,947
[Melissa laughs]

123
00:06:05,031 --> 00:06:06,282
It's a museum.

124
00:06:06,366 --> 00:06:07,366
[Melissa laughs]

125
00:06:08,242 --> 00:06:11,412
It actually looks excellent.
I mean, for museum food...

126
00:06:12,747 --> 00:06:13,581
Whoa.

127
00:06:14,290 --> 00:06:15,958
[Jerry] Ever seen my bit about chopsticks?

128
00:06:16,292 --> 00:06:19,253
Well, I'm sure the Chinese
have seen the fork.

129
00:06:19,670 --> 00:06:21,964
But they said,
"We're staying with the sticks.

130
00:06:22,048 --> 00:06:22,882
[Melissa laughs]

131
00:06:23,257 --> 00:06:26,135
-Yes, obviously, that's better.
-[Melissa laughs]

132
00:06:26,219 --> 00:06:29,263
But we're just gonna ignore it,
and we're gonna stay with sticks."

133
00:06:29,764 --> 00:06:31,307
[Melissa laughs]

134
00:06:31,390 --> 00:06:33,893
How did you learn to do Natalie Portman?

135
00:06:34,227 --> 00:06:35,686
I watched Garden State.

136
00:06:35,770 --> 00:06:39,190
She did that thing where she's just,
"You wanna listen to some music?

137
00:06:39,649 --> 00:06:40,858
I like music.

138
00:06:42,902 --> 00:06:44,570
Everything's so amazing.

139
00:06:45,947 --> 00:06:49,450
I... I... I... Things are just so crazy."

140
00:06:51,577 --> 00:06:53,204
You know, Kristen Wiig, too.

141
00:06:53,287 --> 00:06:54,956
-That's a subtle one also.
-Mm-hmm.

142
00:06:55,414 --> 00:06:56,749
And that one is just so...

143
00:06:57,458 --> 00:07:00,044
"I'm having such a great time. [giggles]

144
00:07:00,503 --> 00:07:03,631
I... I love this rice. It's so good."

145
00:07:03,923 --> 00:07:05,800
[Jerry] Wow! MSG in there?

146
00:07:06,175 --> 00:07:07,844
-[John] A little bit.
-I love MSG.

147
00:07:07,927 --> 00:07:09,345
-[laughs] I love MSG, too.
-Yeah.

148
00:07:09,429 --> 00:07:11,264
Reminds me of Madison Square Garden.

149
00:07:11,431 --> 00:07:12,598
[Melissa laughs]

150
00:07:15,184 --> 00:07:16,561
Do you like to go to movies?

151
00:07:16,894 --> 00:07:19,981
I've been trying to get better at it,
though, going to the movie theater.

152
00:07:20,064 --> 00:07:20,982
Is that a skill?

153
00:07:21,399 --> 00:07:23,693
[Jerry] I don't expect movies
to be that good.

154
00:07:23,776 --> 00:07:25,695
I like going.

155
00:07:26,195 --> 00:07:28,364
When you're going to the movies,

156
00:07:28,448 --> 00:07:30,324
like, you and your friend say,
"Okay, lets go.

157
00:07:30,408 --> 00:07:31,826
-All right, we're gonna go."
-Yeah.

158
00:07:31,909 --> 00:07:33,995
-That is such a happy time.
-Mm-hmm.

159
00:07:34,078 --> 00:07:36,706
-And then you get in the car...
-You get popcorn.

160
00:07:36,789 --> 00:07:39,417
You get popcorn, you walk in.
You get your ticket.

161
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:41,669
-That's the best part.
-Yeah, it is.

162
00:07:41,752 --> 00:07:43,129
The movie, who cares?

163
00:07:44,630 --> 00:07:46,716
-Are you still hungry?
-Yeah.

164
00:07:46,799 --> 00:07:48,885
-Really?
-I saved my appetite for this.

165
00:07:48,968 --> 00:07:51,053
-We didn't eat that much at the museum.
-No.

166
00:07:51,137 --> 00:07:54,932
'Cause it's uncomfortable to eat
in a museum!

167
00:07:58,561 --> 00:08:01,481
And what about your family?
Do you get to see them?

168
00:08:01,564 --> 00:08:03,733
All the time.
I'm real close to my family.

169
00:08:03,816 --> 00:08:05,693
Oh, that's nice. I can feel that.

170
00:08:05,776 --> 00:08:08,988
That's where you get your, uh, security
and your...

171
00:08:09,572 --> 00:08:11,741
your general comfort about yourself.

172
00:08:23,294 --> 00:08:25,171
You know what's weird about Brooklyn?

173
00:08:25,963 --> 00:08:28,090
Every day is Saturday in Brooklyn.

174
00:08:28,174 --> 00:08:30,092
[Melissa laughs]

175
00:08:30,468 --> 00:08:33,512
-It is. Like, go to work!
-It's weird. It's creepy.

176
00:08:33,596 --> 00:08:35,515
I'm gonna go up to people,
"What are you doing?

177
00:08:35,598 --> 00:08:37,683
-Get back to work!"
-[Melissa laughs]

178
00:08:37,767 --> 00:08:39,227
And they go, "I am working."

179
00:08:39,310 --> 00:08:40,686
-"I'm working on my phone."
-Yeah.

180
00:08:40,770 --> 00:08:41,771
[Melissa laughs]

181
00:08:41,854 --> 00:08:44,023
"I'm a graphic artist.
I was just thinking of shapes."

182
00:08:44,106 --> 00:08:44,941
[Melissa laughs]

183
00:08:48,653 --> 00:08:52,531
There's ones I can't say
have, like, the Silly Putty stuff.

184
00:08:52,740 --> 00:08:56,285
And a girl's nails will just, like,
go in the Silly Putty, and she'll just...

185
00:08:57,078 --> 00:08:59,080
-[Jerry] Oh, yeah.
-I like that. That's nice.

186
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:01,374
Silly Putty was my favorite growing up.

187
00:09:01,457 --> 00:09:03,376
-Really?
-I liked Silly Putty and Play-Doh.

188
00:09:03,459 --> 00:09:05,002
Oh, I love Silly Putty, too.

189
00:09:05,086 --> 00:09:08,714
It just seemed like you were gonna be able
to do a lot of great things with it,

190
00:09:08,798 --> 00:09:10,424
-even though you never did.
-Right.

191
00:09:10,508 --> 00:09:12,677
-The package was great.
-Yeah.

192
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:15,263
-The font of Silly Putty was great.
-Yeah.

193
00:09:15,596 --> 00:09:18,558
It just seemed like a whole world
was gonna open up for you.

194
00:09:18,641 --> 00:09:22,019
The more sophisticated
our toys have become...

195
00:09:22,103 --> 00:09:24,397
-The less fun.
-...the less fun we're having.

196
00:09:27,775 --> 00:09:29,527
I have a Steve Buscemi impression.

197
00:09:29,610 --> 00:09:31,821
Just the way he is really calms me.

198
00:09:31,904 --> 00:09:34,699
He has this way of just going with it.

199
00:09:34,782 --> 00:09:36,242
The flow of life, just...

200
00:09:38,703 --> 00:09:39,579
"This is it."

201
00:09:40,413 --> 00:09:43,666
Like, I try to embody him in my life
when people make me feel bad.

202
00:09:43,749 --> 00:09:46,419
Like when my friends say, "Why would you
let your gray hair grow out?"

203
00:09:46,502 --> 00:09:47,420
I could just go...

204
00:09:47,503 --> 00:09:48,629
[Jerry laughs]

205
00:09:48,713 --> 00:09:50,256
"Sometimes I want to look like a skunk."

206
00:09:51,507 --> 00:09:52,425
[Melissa laughs]

207
00:09:55,845 --> 00:09:59,223
-So what happened with the guy?
-I went to Mexico with him.

208
00:09:59,307 --> 00:10:01,225
We were on one of those romantic boats.

209
00:10:01,309 --> 00:10:02,226
-Right.
-At sunset.

210
00:10:02,310 --> 00:10:04,603
And I realized he didn't have
the same sense of humor as me,

211
00:10:04,687 --> 00:10:07,565
because I saw his...
the little top of his...

212
00:10:08,524 --> 00:10:10,443
crack, and I just went, "boop."

213
00:10:10,526 --> 00:10:13,863
And he got so mad. He was like,
"Don't do that. I don't like that."

214
00:10:13,946 --> 00:10:17,825
I called him after the trip ended.
I said, "Look, it's not gonna work out.

215
00:10:18,075 --> 00:10:20,870
We don't have the same sense of humor
and that's really important to me."

216
00:10:20,953 --> 00:10:23,789
"I don't like that. My Dad did that to me
growing up. I don't like that."

217
00:10:23,873 --> 00:10:24,832
And I said...

218
00:10:25,625 --> 00:10:29,086
"Well, your dad sounds great!
Is he still with your mom?" [laughs]

219
00:10:32,381 --> 00:10:34,467
-I have a Sandra Bullock impression.
-[Jerry] Really?

220
00:10:34,550 --> 00:10:36,886
I say this is her knocking over dominoes.

221
00:10:36,969 --> 00:10:39,263
She just goes, "No, no, no, no,
no, no, no." And that's it.

222
00:10:39,347 --> 00:10:41,098
[Melissa laughs]

223
00:10:42,850 --> 00:10:45,144
-It's been fun to share.
-Fun to share.

224
00:10:45,227 --> 00:10:47,855
-[Melissa] I think it's--
-I feel like I'm watching Mister Rogers.

225
00:10:47,938 --> 00:10:49,148
-But I--
-Can you do him?

226
00:10:49,398 --> 00:10:52,735
No. And actually,
I started the movie last night.

227
00:10:52,818 --> 00:10:53,819
I didn't get to see it.

228
00:10:53,903 --> 00:10:55,738
[Jerry] It's a great movie, but...

229
00:10:55,821 --> 00:10:59,700
it makes you feel like you're
a really mean person compared to him.

230
00:10:59,784 --> 00:11:01,577
[Melissa laughs]

231
00:11:01,952 --> 00:11:05,331
Mister Rogers makes you feel
really bad about yourself.

232
00:11:05,414 --> 00:11:07,333
The opposite of what he's trying to do.

233
00:11:07,416 --> 00:11:08,751
Right. [laughs]

234
00:11:08,834 --> 00:11:10,670
It didn't make me feel good at all.

235
00:11:11,796 --> 00:11:14,340
-I feel, like, whoa!
-The same for me. Like, "Whoa, I'm...

236
00:11:14,423 --> 00:11:16,509
-Yeah, "I got some work to do." Yeah.
-...the devil."

237
00:11:16,592 --> 00:11:17,968
"I am not that nice.

238
00:11:18,052 --> 00:11:19,053
[Melissa laughs]

239
00:11:19,136 --> 00:11:20,930
Jeez, I was not aware of that."

240
00:11:21,013 --> 00:11:22,223
[Melissa laughs]

241
00:11:23,307 --> 00:11:24,308
Face!

242
00:11:24,392 --> 00:11:25,643
[both laughing]

243
00:11:29,980 --> 00:11:32,191
Somebody dropped their wrapping paper.
Did you see that?

244
00:11:32,775 --> 00:11:34,527
Someone's gonna have
a bad gift experience.

245
00:11:34,610 --> 00:11:35,611
[Melissa laughs]

246
00:11:35,945 --> 00:11:38,572
"What is it? Oh, I see what it is."

247
00:11:41,701 --> 00:11:43,953
Did you have a favorite roller coaster
when you were a kid?

248
00:11:44,036 --> 00:11:45,955
I don't like roller coasters.
They scare me.

249
00:11:46,038 --> 00:11:46,872
Okay. [laughs]

250
00:11:47,456 --> 00:11:50,334
-What is the big--
-I love that you know yourself so well.

251
00:11:50,835 --> 00:11:53,421
-God!
-What is the big deal in scaring people?

252
00:11:53,504 --> 00:11:54,964
It's so easy.

253
00:11:55,047 --> 00:11:57,925
I don't know why they make scary movies
and scary roller coasters,

254
00:11:58,008 --> 00:11:59,301
like that's hard to do.

255
00:11:59,635 --> 00:12:02,888
-We're half terrified every second anyway.
-I know, I know.

256
00:12:02,972 --> 00:12:05,725
We're all little lemurs
here on the planet just...

257
00:12:06,225 --> 00:12:09,270
Darting eyes and freaking out
at every little sound.

258
00:12:09,353 --> 00:12:10,813
"Hey, what if we scared you?"

259
00:12:10,896 --> 00:12:11,981
[Melissa laughs]

260
00:12:26,162 --> 00:12:28,122
Look at this guy. Here's a guy doing it.

261
00:12:28,205 --> 00:12:31,751
The two kids, the wife, the backpack.
He's double-parked.

262
00:12:32,042 --> 00:12:35,129
He's got the door open,
people are trying to get by. He's dealing.

263
00:12:35,212 --> 00:12:38,382
Plus, he's got two different
facial hair lengths he's gotta manage.

264
00:12:38,466 --> 00:12:39,467
[Melissa laughs]

265
00:12:39,550 --> 00:12:40,801
-[Jerry laughs]
-Oh, gosh.

266
00:12:41,135 --> 00:12:42,595
I like a guy like that.

267
00:12:43,304 --> 00:12:44,346
Doing it all.

